WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:05.200
If you're going to tell the story of a&nbsp;
metal drummer who's losing his hearing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:08.720
how do you dramatize that and connect the&nbsp;
audience with the character's experience?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:13.760
One of the beautiful things about film is that&nbsp;
as a medium that combines both sound and picture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:17.200
it's actually possible to modify&nbsp;
the sound the audience is hearing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:20.080
so that it mimics the perspective&nbsp;
of someone with hearing loss.

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:24.960
[Inaudible muffled sound]

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:28.640
For director Darius Maurder and&nbsp;
supervising sound editor Nicholas Becker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:32.800
these representations of Ruben's hearing&nbsp;
loss were based on extensive research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:38.400
into how hearing loss sounds, that started a year&nbsp;
before any other crew were brought onto the film. &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:40.515
This video is sponsored by Nordpass.

00:00:40.515 --> 00:00:44.001
Creating&nbsp;the soundscape of deafness is extremely nuanced.

00:00:44.001 --> 00:00:48.223
But there are a few simple techniques that are&nbsp;
used to great effect in the film

00:00:48.223 --> 00:00:53.928
THOMAS : This is Alex Knickerbocker. And chances are you've probably&nbsp;heard his work at some point watching tv or in a theater.

00:00:53.928 --> 00:01:01.384
THOMAS: I've learned a lot about film sound&nbsp;from his channel and so I reached out to see if he would help me understand the artistry that&nbsp;went into the sound of "The Sound of Metal."

00:01:01.384 --> 00:01:05.726
The two biggest factors in how we perceive sound are&nbsp;
frequency and amplitude.

00:01:05.726 --> 00:01:11.840
THOMAS: In moments representing the beginning of Ruben's hearing loss things don't&nbsp;just sound quieter but are duller and muffled

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:17.040
[low rumbling - muffled dialogue]

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:21.760
ALEX: As we start losing our hearing we're not just&nbsp;
losing all frequencies at the same rate.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:26.400
It's not&nbsp;like turning a volume knob down we're actually&nbsp;
losing each individual frequency differently.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:27.910
RUBEN: [muffled] I can't hear anything.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.360
THOMAS: most of what Ruben can still hear&nbsp;
are lower end frequencies and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:35.120
these frequencies are what we get to hear as&nbsp;
the audience when we're in his perspective.

00:01:35.958 --> 00:01:37.077
RUBEN: [muffled] I can hear you.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:41.428
THOMAS: Some of the work to create this effect can be done&nbsp;
in post-production.

00:01:41.428 --> 00:01:47.600
ALEX: You can mimic that simply by recording your voice into a normal microphone&nbsp;and rolling off all the high frequencies until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:49.613
only those lower frequencies are left.

00:01:49.613 --> 00:01:55.440
THOMAS: for example i could use an equalizer on my voice to cut out the high end and make&nbsp;it sound more muffled like you're hearing now. [sound becomes muffled]&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:59.840
THOMAS: But creating this effect as accurately and&nbsp;
richly as possible starts even in how you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:01.703
approach recording the sound.

00:02:01.703 --> 00:02:11.042
ALEX: There's a type of&nbsp;microphone called a contact mic and it picks up sound not through the air as a normal microphone&nbsp;
would but through vibrations in solid objects.

00:02:11.142 --> 00:02:16.080
ALEX: So the microphone needs to be in direct contact with&nbsp;
whatever object it's going to record sound through.

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:21.200
[low metallic drumming]

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:24.640
ALEX: So what you end up with is a very&nbsp;
different perception of sound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:29.749
because you're not hearing it travel through&nbsp;
air as normal it's coming through very condensed&nbsp;solid matter.

00:02:29.844 --> 00:02:33.680
 THOMAS: For example in this scene a contact&nbsp;
mic placed directly on the slide would have a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:38.160
different perception of the sound of Ruben&nbsp;
drumming than a typical microphone would.&nbsp;

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:41.670
[low metallic drumming]

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:46.080
This difference in perception that&nbsp;
can be captured with a contact mic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:50.400
was an important piece of illustrating Rubin's&nbsp;
experience of hearing loss for the audience.

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:58.560
For Nicholas Becker sometimes even the&nbsp;
existing recording equipment wasn't enough.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:03.520
"I was able to build a small microphone that you&nbsp;
can put in your mouth without it being broken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:07.040
we were able to really recall&nbsp;
all the inner sounds from Riz&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:10.880
racing heartbeats blood pressure&nbsp;
tendons moves muscle and bone movements."

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:16.962
[low rumbling + clicking and popping sounds]

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:20.560
THOMAS: But creating a compelling sound experience&nbsp;
for the audience and telling the film's story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:25.600
wasn't just about being able to create an accurate&nbsp;
representation of reuben's subjective perspective.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:30.880
One of the most important elements is how the&nbsp;
film uses the movement between sonic perspectives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:33.840
and the contrast between loud&nbsp;
and quiet moments in the film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:38.695
This is ridiculous

00:03:38.695 --> 00:03:43.982
ALEX: One of my favorite sonic&nbsp;choices throughout the entire film is the use of contrast,

00:03:43.982 --> 00:03:49.120
between perspectives between loud&nbsp;
versus quiet between delicate versus intense.

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:51.840
[loud drumming, electric guitar]

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:55.360
ALEX: With a film titled the sound&nbsp;
of metal you expect that to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:59.840
abrasive to some degree you expect&nbsp;
it to be really over the top

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:07.840
The opening sequence of the concert is just that&nbsp;
it's extremely aggressive it's really intense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:12.050
sound and then the second you get away from that&nbsp;
it cuts to...

00:04:12.965 --> 00:04:17.832
ALEX: A peaceful morning inside of basically a camper out in the middle of a parking lot.

00:04:17.832 --> 00:04:25.200
Using&nbsp;that kind of contrast just in perspective changes really draws your attention to how loud&nbsp;
something is or how aggressive something&nbsp;is&nbsp;

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:30.240
and then gives you a point of reference that's&nbsp;
totally separate from what that was just like so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:33.205
it guides the audience's attention very&nbsp;
effectively.

00:04:33.205 --> 00:04:40.000
THOMAS: That contrast between the loud concert and the quiet environment the next day makes&nbsp;us more aware of the sound in the camper and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:43.920
Ruben's environment, without drawing too&nbsp;
much attention to the sound design itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:45.920
[loud breaths - coffee brewing]

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:49.600
ALEX: You have all of these little&nbsp;
subtle details of the birds outside... 

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:51.680
[seagulls - distant traffic]

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:55.360
ALEX: The atmospheres feel just a&nbsp;
little bit more pristine...&nbsp;

00:04:55.360 --> 00:05:00.800
And even down to the foley or the production&nbsp;
recordings of the blender going in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:05.953
morning and all the little details of the foley&nbsp;
as the characters move around the trailer itself. &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.953 --> 00:05:11.423
[cloth rustling - creaking]

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:14.880
ALEX:  It's all this stuff that you&nbsp;
wouldn't ordinarily think of.

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:21.280
Drawing our attention to these sounds&nbsp;
is important because it increases our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:25.440
awareness of their absence the next day&nbsp;
as Ruben begins to lose his hearing.

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:30.320
[muffled rumbling]

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:35.120
The same idea can be applied when you're&nbsp;
switching back and forth between perspectives from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:39.615
the you know normal perception of of the world&nbsp;
around you.

00:05:39.615 --> 00:05:46.240
PHARMACIST: It's probably a two out of ten in terms of his hearing he can't he's having&nbsp;
trouble even communicating with me. 

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:49.744
ALEX: Versus&nbsp;what it's like to perceive those same&nbsp;
things without being able to hear them.

00:05:49.744 --> 00:05:54.683
[muffled rumbling - inaudible dialogue]

00:05:54.800 --> 00:06:01.189
ALEX:  Having that contrast makes the starkness of&nbsp;
silence that much more intense and that much&nbsp;more heavy.

00:06:01.189 --> 00:06:05.840
 THOMAS: The film spends a lot of time inside&nbsp;
Ruben's auditory perspective at the beginning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:11.120
often tying these moments to more subjective shots&nbsp;
that places inside Ruben's perspective visually.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:16.400
But as the story moves along more time is&nbsp;
spent back in the auditory perspective of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:21.257
full hearing and the film only uses Ruben's&nbsp;
auditory perspective for emphasis. 

00:06:23.048 --> 00:06:28.640
THOMAS: But because&nbsp;the film returns mostly to the perspective of full&nbsp;
hearing during Ruben's stay at the deaf community...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.650 --> 00:06:31.011
What?

00:06:33.084 --> 00:06:36.480
THOMAS: There are lots of scenes&nbsp;
with no dialogue or music.

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:41.120
THOMAS: And because of that the sounds from&nbsp;
the environment and the movements of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:45.680
the characters take more of a center&nbsp;
stage than they would in most films.

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:53.200
ALEX: The sound team did a really effective job with&nbsp;
this film in the quiet and subtle moments because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:58.080
from a technical perspective once you start&nbsp;
taking away music you take away a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:02.320
you know over-the-top action stuff, sonically&nbsp;
there's not really a whole lot to hide behind.

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:10.400
So you better make sure that your production&nbsp;
sound is really clean you don't have any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:13.920
traffic, you don't have anything that&nbsp;
isn't going on in picture to distract,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:19.200
and you have to make sure that all of the&nbsp;
background layers of sounds, from the birds outside...

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:29.840
...to maybe the creaking of the old house...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:34.800
...to the wind coming through you&nbsp;
know maybe a drafty point a window...

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:42.960
...all of those little details need to be&nbsp;
exactly right. One of the hardest balances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:48.000
to find in those moments is how loud you&nbsp;
play certain foley movements you know even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:52.640
the smallest most delicate cloth movements,&nbsp;
if you don't hear those to a degree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:57.520
a film sounds lifeless and you don't notice&nbsp;
it until it's not there. So during these scenes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:01.840
when you have a whole group of people not&nbsp;
talking they're just signing to each other...

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:07.840
...the foley of cloth movement...

00:08:10.697 --> 00:08:13.760
...the foley of the actual sound of your hands.

00:08:15.570 --> 00:08:18.945
You&nbsp;
know if you get really close into a microphone... [hands rustling]

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:22.720
You can hear that. You can hear that there&nbsp;
is some movement to it, but obviously if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:25.680
overplay that it's going to sound&nbsp;
unnatural and draw attention to itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:30.800
So that balance that they struck in those&nbsp;
scenes had to have been extremely delicate.

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:32.800
[wind - quiet creaking screen door]

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:38.480
THOMAS: Ultimately the sound design plays a big role in&nbsp;
the emotional story arc and Ruben's character arc&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.480 --> 00:08:44.240
in the film. The contrast we talked about earlier&nbsp;
of the loud distorted sounds of the metal concert,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.240 --> 00:08:48.000
against both the quieter sounds of&nbsp;
the next day, and Ruben's hearing loss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:54.160
sets up a kind of sonic motif that bookends&nbsp;
the story. Spoilers for the end of the movie&nbsp;ahead. 

00:08:54.465 --> 00:08:58.720
Once Rubin gets cochlear implants the&nbsp;
film's interpretation of what this sounds like.

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:04.364
[metallic distorted city sounds]

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:08.880
THOMAS: While less pleasant and musical than&nbsp;
the concert music the film opens with...

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:11.906
[loud distorted metallic guitar]

00:09:11.906 --> 00:09:13.906
[loud metallic bell striking]

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:20.000
...bears some striking resemblance nonetheless.&nbsp;
Through distortion and compression everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:26.640
sounds overwhelming, metallic, and grating. At&nbsp;
both the beginning and the end of the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:33.200
there's a shift from a sound of metal, to quiet.&nbsp;
And it's Ruben's change in how he perceives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:37.465
these two shifts that marks his character's&nbsp;
development.

00:09:37.465 --> 00:09:39.120
The first shift is something lost

00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:44.575
And the second through the acceptance Ruben&nbsp;
has learned is something gained.   &nbsp;

00:09:44.575 --> 00:09:46.390
[total silence]

